ave rocket), but avoid overcommitting high cards unless necessary.\n\nBest strategy: Beat the landlord efficiently without wasting power. Playing J-J-J with a low attached pair like 3s or 6s conserves stronger combinations for later.\n\nBut note: We also have two 2s left (♠2, ♣2, ♦2 — wait, we have three 2s!), and 2s are very valuable. Using two of them now in a pair might weaken future endgame control.\n\nWe hold ♠2, ♣2, ♦2 — that’s three 2s. If we use two here, we leave one, which may limit bomb potential (need four 2s for bomb). So using 2s as a pair is inefficient — better to save them for possible bombs or final plays.\n\nSimilarly, Q-Q is useful — we only have two Qs, so using them here burns our only pair of Qs.\n\nUsing 3s or 6s is better: we have pairs of both, and they’re low, less useful otherwise.\n\nHowever, playing J-J-J with 3s or 6s is fine — but look: the move ♠3 ♥3 ♠J ♥J ♣J uses up both 3s and all three Js — acceptable since we don’t need multiple copies.\n\nBut more importantly: by playing a high triplet like J, we are already beating the landlord sufficiently. No need to go higher.\n\nNow, compare impact:\n- Playing J-J-J + 3s: uses low-value pair, saves 6s, Qs, and 2s → good\n- Playing J-J-J + 6s: similar, but 6s slightly more valuable than 3s? Not really — both are low\n\nBut wait — do we want to help our teammate? They have 17 cards — likely holding mid-to-high cards. By not going too high, we allow our teammate to potentially take control later.\n\nSo best to use the minimal sufficient upgrade: beat 8s with J-J-J and a low attached pair.\n\nBetween 3s and 6s, either is fine. But 3s are lower-ranked, so marginally better to use them first.\n\nThus, best move: ♠3 ♥3 ♠J ♥J ♣J\n\nBut wait — is this combination actually in the valid moves? Yes: "triple_with_pair: ♠3 ♥3 ♠J ♥J ♣J" is listed.\n\nAlternatively, could we pass? Passing is allowed, but unwise here:\n- We have a clear, efficient beat.\n- Passing would let teammate carry burden, but they may not have a higher triple+pair.\n- Landlord will continue building momentum.\n\nSo we should play.\n\nReason:  \nBeating the landlord’s triple_with_pair efficiently preserves high-value cards (like 2s and Qs) for later critical moments. Playing J-J-J with 3s uses a minimal overkill while maintaining strategic flexibility. It avoids wasteful use of scarce pairs (Qs, 2s) and keeps pressure on the landlord without overextending.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠3", "♥3", "♠J", "♥J", "♣J"]}'}
